  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Stalked hairy fairy cup (Lachnum virgineum) is a small, cup-shaped fungus belonging to the Lachnaceae family, characterized by its delicate white or pale fruiting bodies. Despite its common name suggesting a plant, it is actually an ascomycete fungus found throughout New England on decaying wood and plant debris.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cup-shaped fruiting body",
      "description": "Distinctive small, open cups or saucers typically 2-5mm in diameter with a pale white to cream color",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Hairy exterior",
      "description": "The outer surface is covered with fine, hair-like setae (bristles) that give it a fuzzy appearance under magnification",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stalked base",
      "description": "Each fruiting body sits on a slender stem or stalk, distinguishing it from sessile (stalkless) cup fungi",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Lachnum virgineum grows on moist, decaying hardwood, leaf litter, and rotting wood in shaded forest environments throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'fairy cup' comes from folklore and whimsy, as these tiny cup-shaped fungi were historically imagined as miniature vessels used by fairies in enchanted forests.",
